# Target C: profile-flexible maximum population and complete fixed-wall exclusion

Date: 2026-10-07. Authority: main
`4b0b982451effe3e8183edfce349ce4c25f9a3bb`, audit.74.
**Proved candidate awaiting independent audit.** No self-promotion.

Provenance: Worker 1's immutable tip
`bf288c82e8a62466d5580025c94e750a177e63e2` contains
`target_c_sorted_rate_packet_classification_2026-10-06.md`. Its finite
classification is unreviewed. This report independently rederives the
full-chart implication needed here, fixes its scopes explicitly, and
extends the proof from arithmetic rates to any fixed distinct symmetric
profile. The new one-deficit sector-peeling theorem is a separate input.
No central-vacancy classification is needed or asserted.

## 1. Statement and terminal relevance

Fix M>=3 and strictly decreasing symmetric real rates
a_0>...>a_(M-1), a_(M-1-h)=-a_h. Construct the six-direction wall by
the sorted ascending unit factors exp(i*a_h*theta) on each side. Put
N=3M^2-3M+1. For every sufficiently small theta>0, **every** N-point
unit-separated interior cloud in this wall is exactly a sorting packet
for a permutation of these rates, after relabeling. In particular it
contains an actual unit equilateral triangle.

Combining this with the new one-deficit packet excludes the entire
specified wall class in a canonical triangle-free source for each fixed
M>=4 and each fixed profile, at sufficiently small positive curvature.
For these fixed profiles the finite Oler upper count is z<=N because its
continuous right side tends to N from within the interval (N-1,N+1).
The perimeter includes all 6M old wall labels.

`finite maximum-population classification + all-M one-deficit exchange
-> every allowed interior population excluded -> complete fixed-wall-class
exclusion`.

This is not an arbitrary macroscopic-container extraction theorem. No
onset uniform in growing M or profiles is supplied. No globally optimal
triangle-free family is constructed. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 2. Localization and O(theta) displacement, with no source deletion

The wall tends to the regular hexagon H_M. Adding its 6M wall vertices
to the N interior points attains the limiting Oler count
3M^2+3M+1. The Oler equality argument audited in audit.74 depends only
on this flat limiting wall and this count, not on arithmetic spacing.
Its reliable equilateral-triangulation equality statement fixes the
complete lattice chart P_M; hence the interior limit is P_(M-1).
This imports only the independently promoted equality implication, and
explicitly repeats its applicability for the present profiles.

Label the old interior by that limit. Its displacement delta from the
flat chart is O(theta). To prove this rate, suppose |delta|/theta tends
to infinity along a subsequence. Normalize delta by its Euclidean norm.
All wall shifts are O(theta); after division they disappear. The limiting
unit-pair inequalities then give a nonzero feasible infinitesimal motion
of the full flat triangular interior with its complete wall fixed.

That static cone is zero: weight one on every flat unit edge involving
a moving site equilibrates at every moving site (all its six neighbours
are retained in the complete chart). Its positive weighted sum forces
all first rows to be equalities. Pinned triangular rigidity, proved by
propagating rigidity across the connected elementary-triangle complex
from fixed wall triangles, forces the displacement to be zero. This is
a theorem about the auxiliary complete chart, not full-source ZR/ZF.
It proves the required contradiction and the O(theta) rate.

Thus every sequence of feasible clouds has a subsequence
delta=theta*d+o(theta). The following analysis classifies every such d.

## 3. The complete first-order polytope

Write u=(1,0), v=(1/2,sqrt(3)/2), c=sqrt(3)/2. On ring r every ring
edge is a strong zero first row. So is every radial unit edge along the
six corner chains, including the corner guards at radius M. These
equalities follow from the nested flat positive stresses: the ring-r
stress has weight one on that ring and the six outward radial chains.
All its pinned endpoints are corners whose first wall shifts vanish.
Each of its lower first rows is nonnegative, so each is zero. Use these
stresses separately, not as additive energy currency.

The center velocity is zero. Corner chain constraints make each axis
velocity tangential; the straight ring sides make its tangential
coefficient the same on the six corners of each ring. Write

`d(r*e_k)=alpha_r*J(e_k)`, alpha_0=alpha_M=0,
`t_h=alpha_(h+1)-alpha_h`, sum t_h=0.

At sector site p=(r-j)u+jv, the ring equality gives

`d(p) dot u=c*s_(r,j)`,
`d(p) dot v=c*(s_(r,j)+alpha_r)`.

The axis values are s_(r,0)=0 and s_(r,r)=-alpha_r. The two other
flat nearest-neighbour lower rows are exactly

`s_(r,j)>=s_(r-1,j)`,
`s_(r,j)>=s_(r-1,j-1)-t_(r-1)`.

Let B_j be the sum of the j smallest prescribed rates. The sorted
outer wall has first boundary value s_(M,j)=-B_j. Every j-element
subset I of the M radial increments can be chosen by a path from the
center to this boundary. The two recurrences imply

`sum_(h in I) t_h >= B_j`, for every |I|=j.

These subset inequalities, together with sum t=0, say exactly that t
belongs to the convex hull of the permutations of a. One direct proof
sorts the coefficients of any linear functional and uses Abel summation:
the subset bounds, including complementary subsets, bound it by its
rearrangement maximum on a permutation of a. Separation gives inclusion
in that convex hull. Conversely the prefix choice

`s_(r,j)=-sum of the j smallest (t_0,...,t_(r-1))`

satisfies both recurrences at interior layers. At the outer layer use
the prescribed value -B_j; the subset bounds ensure both entering
inequalities. Rotation makes the axis definitions agree. Thus the
projected first-order polytope is exactly this permutahedron.

The full first-order polytope P in moving Cartesian coordinates is
bounded: its recession cone is precisely the zero static cone from
Section 2. Its affine hull L is the strong-row zero space. Every other
row is strictly positive at some sorting permutation (place the new
rate above or below the competing prefix rate). Averaging all permutation
fields is strictly positive on every weak row. Therefore there is no
unmentioned universal equality restricting L.

## 4. One energy sum forces a vertex and its complete field

Use just the central six-chain positive stress. Its exact finite
weighted unit inequalities cancel every moving linear term. The six
outer corners are L(theta)*e_k, where

`L(theta)=sum cos(a_h*theta)=M-(theta^2/2)*sum a_h^2+O(theta^4)`.

Their linear contribution is 12*(L(theta)-M). Consequently

`sum_chain |delta_p-delta_q|^2 >= 12*(M-L(theta))`.

Divide by theta^2. The chain energy is 6*sum t_h^2, so
sum t_h^2>=sum a_h^2. On the permutahedron the reverse inequality
holds by convexity of squared norm. Strict convexity shows equality
occurs only at a permutation of a. No C2 expansion of the old cloud
is assumed; theta*d+o(theta) suffices.

The complete field is unique over a permutation t*. The recurrences
give the prefix-smallest formula as a lower bound on s_(r,j). For its
upper bound choose a threshold between the jth and (j+1)th entries of
the prefix. Extend to the outer wall by selecting exactly the suffix
rates below that threshold. The selected prefix and suffix together
are the smallest j+k full rates. The outer inequality is an equality
at a permutation, giving exactly the matching upper bound. Axis cases
j=0,r have the already fixed values. Hence every sector and coordinate
is forced. Only the finitely many permutation fields survive.

## 5. The finite sorting packet and its active rows

For such a permutation t*, put z_h=exp(i*t_h*theta), S_r=sum_(h<r)z_h,
and U_(r,j)=the sum of the j smallest-rate factors in that prefix. Define

`Q_(r,j)(theta)=S_r*u+U_(r,j)*(v-u)`.

Rotate through six sectors, and retain the center. At radius M this
is the prescribed wall. Ring differences are unit factors times v-u.
For each successive layer one of the other two neighbour differences
is a unit factor times u or v; the other has squared length
2-2*cos(pi/3+|t_new-t_old|*theta)>1. All nonnearest pairs have strict
flat gaps. This proves exact feasibility and identifies *all* active
rows J for sufficiently small theta. J is exactly the set of rows
whose first-order slack at d* is zero.

The limiting moving-coordinate matrix R_0 on J has full column rank.
If x were in its kernel, d*+epsilon*x and d*-epsilon*x would both lie
in P for sufficiently small epsilon, since all inactive weak rows have
strict slack. But the central energy gradient g_0=grad E_0(d*) has d*
as its unique maximizer on P: in rate coordinates its value is
12*sum t*_h*t_h, maximized uniquely at t=t*, and Section 4 forces the
fiber over t*. This contradiction proves full rank. It is an exact
argument for every fixed M and permutation, not a generic rank claim.

## 6. Positive stress continuation and local isolation

Because d* uniquely maximizes g_0 on the polytope, g_0 restricted to L
lies in the relative interior of its normal cone at d*. This relative
interior is open in L*: P is full dimensional in L by Section 3.

Start with the central chain stress and add sufficiently small positive
multiples of every other nested stress. Denote their sum by lambda_0.
It is positive on every strong row and zero on every weak row, with
R_0^T*lambda_0=0. Its energy gradient g remains in that same normal-cone
interior because the added energies perturb g_0 continuously. This use
of stresses constructs positive coefficients; it does not sum energy
lower bounds.

Let R_theta=R_0+theta*R_1+O(theta^2) be the actual sorting contact matrix.
The gradient identity is g=2*R_1^T*lambda_0 in moving coordinates.
The normal-cone formula for lower inequalities is
`g=-R_0^T*mu`, modulo strong-row normals, with nonnegative weak mu.
Interior membership allows coefficients positive on *every* active weak
row: subtract a sufficiently small positive sum of those outward normals
from g and represent the remainder in the cone. Strong coefficients
may have either sign because they represent the affine-hull normal.
Consequently there is lambda_1, strictly positive on every active weak
row, satisfying

`R_0^T*lambda_1=-R_1^T*lambda_0`.

Since R_0 has full column rank, R_theta^T has a bounded analytic right
inverse at theta=0. Correct the O(theta^2) residual of
lambda_0+theta*lambda_1 using that inverse. The resulting exact pinned
stress lambda_theta satisfies R_theta^T*lambda_theta=0, is positive
on every contact, is bounded above, and has every coefficient at least
c_1*theta for small theta. Strong coefficients remain positive by their
zeroth-order margins; weak coefficients do so by their first-order
margins. All constants are local to this fixed M, profile and permutation.

For any other feasible placement at displacement x from this sorting
packet, each active unit row obeys
`(R_theta*x)_e>=-|x_p-x_q|^2/2>=-C*|x|^2`.
The weighted sum of the linear rows is exactly zero. Positivity and
the c_1*theta lower bound bound each row above by C'*|x|^2/theta.
Uniform full column rank then gives

`|x| <= C''*|x|^2/theta`.

Therefore a distinct feasible placement must be at least c_2*theta
away. This proves local isolation at the needed scale; ordinary
qualitative isolation alone would not complete the sequence argument.

## 7. Exhausting the maximum population and the fixed wall class

If finite classification failed for arbitrarily small theta, Sections
2--4 would give a countersequence with a subsequence whose normalized
displacement tends to one permutation field d*. Its corresponding
sorting packet has the same first field, so their difference is o(theta).
Section 6 forces exact equality for all sufficiently late members, a
contradiction. This proves the classification for every sufficiently
small theta, with no selection of a favorable old cloud.

Every sorting packet contains the center and the first ring
z_0*e_k, k=0,...,5. The center and any two adjacent first-ring sites
form an exact unit triangle. They are all interior for M>=2. Thus
the complete N-point old cloud is incompatible with triangle-freeness.

For M>=4, combine this z=N exclusion with
`target_c_all_M_one_deficit_sector_peeling_2026-10-07.md`, which pays
all z<=N-1 without classifying the source. The old wall plus every
old interior label enters the Oler count; the canonical one-deletion
shield retains every exterior isolate in the replacement branch.
All interior populations are excluded for the stated fixed wall family.

Remaining terminal obligations are still substantial: this wall must
be extracted from or replaced by a packet in an arbitrary canonical
macroscopic chamber, with control as M grows; paired exterior caps
need actual exact capacity or retained-core motion; full-core trains
need a source-realized finite move passing all lower and upper blockers.
An exact, identical six-side profile is not inferred from a chamber's
area, near-disk shape, boundary complexity or local curvature.

## 8. An explicit descending-profile first correction

There is an additional direct check on the load-bearing stress bridge.
For the descending profile, start with the principal radial-chain stress
lambda_0 alone. Use Cartesian first velocities, but write
lambda_1=sqrt(3)*kappa. Set kappa=0 on every ring edge. On a radial
axis edge from r to r+1 set kappa=(a_r-a_0)/3. On a unit diagonal
chain (a,b) to (a,b+1), a>=1, set

`kappa=(2/3)*(a_(a-1)-a_a)>0`,

and rotate this assignment through all sectors. At offaxis sites the
two chain coefficients are equal and their limiting vectors opposite;
the force is zero. At axis r the diagonal coefficient contributes
(a_(r-1)-a_r)*Ju, while the radial coefficient difference cancels its
u-component. This is exactly the required negative first force of
the principal chain stress. At the center all coefficients are zero.
Thus R_0^T*lambda_1=-R_1^T*lambda_0 holds for every M and descending
strict profile, with explicitly positive coefficients on every weak row.

Adding sufficiently small positive nested-ring stresses makes lambda_0
positive on all strong rows. Full rank permits a first-correction change
of size O(eta), which preserves the explicit weak signs. This gives a
direct descending-packet stress continuation independently of the
normal-cone representation. Its weak coefficient lower bound is
(2/sqrt(3))*min_h(a_h-a_(h+1)). No uniform bound on the rank inverse,
perturbation size or finite isolation onset is inferred from this alone.

An independent exact certificate also checks first stress corrections
for 14 arithmetic/nonarithmetic profile permutations at M=3,...,6.
Run `python3 research/verify_target_c_profile_first_stress_correction_2026_10_07.py`.
It reconstructs the complete limiting active row set and both force
identities using rational arithmetic, checks positive weak coefficients,
and checks a full modular minor. The input coefficients were discovered
with floating optimization, rounded to denominator 3000000, and projected
onto the exact force equations; all final signs and identities are exact.
The verifier has no solver tolerance or numerical optimization dependency.
Its finite certificate does not replace the all-permutation analytic
argument or prove finite stress continuation by itself.
